check takes 44s on this tracker's 43 tickets. Measured 2026-09-17 on aarch64
Linux by checking out bin/pidly at each commit and running it against the
same tracker:

  after 24 (required means a value)      19s
  after 28 (patterns, schema stanzas)    36s
  after 40 (the format's own lines)      47s

So it roughly doubled while values gained patterns, and the cost is a second
a ticket. On the 2000-ticket tracker the other measurements use, that is
half an hour for the command that is meant to run in CI.

It is process count, not work. Almost all of check's per-ticket cost is
spawning, and three things drive it:

- info_faults is called inside a pipeline — `info_faults ... | while read` —
  so every call runs in its own subshell, and schema_cached and format_cached
  reparse per ticket rather than once per command. The cache is real; the
  subshell defeats it.
- The positional checks read lines 1, 2 and 3 with a sed each. A POSIX shell
  can read three lines from a file with no process at all:
  { read -r a; read -r b; read -r c; } < info
- Every non-empty value with a pattern costs a grep, because grep is the
  matcher (28: mawk has no interval expressions). That is the one real cost,
  and it is per ticket per patterned field.

The first two are straightforward. The third is the interesting one: the
loop could be inverted — one pass collecting every ticket's value for one
field, then a single grep -E over that list — which is O(patterns) greps for
the whole tracker instead of O(tickets x patterns). That changes which
findings are easy to attribute to a ticket, so it wants care.

Done when check on 2000 tickets is measured, the per-ticket process count is
down by an order of magnitude, the findings are unchanged ticket for ticket,
and the suite still passes.


Done 2026-09-18.

Measured on aarch64 Linux, on trackers built by hand rather than by n calls
to new, with every external command wrapped in a script that logs its name
and execs the real one. Counted rather than timed where the claim is about
processes: a stopwatch measures the machine.

  processes, 100 tickets      846        53
  processes, 500 tickets     4230        53
  wall clock, 2000 tickets  7m07s     0m46s
  wall clock, this repo     11.6s      3.7s

Per ticket that is 8.46 processes against 0.53, and the second row is the
part that matters: the count is now the same at 500 tickets as at 100, so
there is no per-ticket term left to grow.

Four things, and the first was the one the ticket had not found:

- Matching is batched. A value needing a pattern tested is queued with the
  finding it would produce, grouped by pattern, and one grep per distinct
  pattern tests every value in the tracker. This is the inversion the ticket
  proposed; what makes it safe is that the report is sorted before it is
  printed, so the order findings are emitted in was never visible. 508 greps
  became 11.
- before() compared two timestamps with printf | sort | head inside a command
  substitution — four processes, once per ticket, to order two strings. Both
  are fixed-width ISO-8601 with their separators in the same places, so it
  walks them a character at a time in the shell. Any position where they
  differ holds a digit in both, so there is no leading zero to be read as
  octal and nothing wide enough to overflow. A value that is not that shape
  still takes the sort.
- missing_fields was an awk per ticket, plus a grep per field to ask whether
  it was required. It is one awk for the whole tracker now, reading each info
  with getline — the same shape the cycle pass already used, six lines below
  it.
- registry_dir was called inside the per-ticket loop, which is a command
  substitution, which is a fork, for a string that cannot change while the
  loop runs.

The ticket's other two bullets were already done: info_faults no longer runs
in a pipeline, and the positional lines are read with three reads rather than
a sed each.

Findings are unchanged. A tracker carrying thirteen faults — pattern misses
on both a project field and a format one, an empty value, updated before
created, a line 3 that is not title:, a dangling need, a field not in the
schema, an unparsable claim, a non-field line, a phantom directory, and the
registry wrong in both directions — reports byte-identically before and
after.

tests/cost.sh is the guard, and it guards the risk this change introduces
rather than the symptom. A batch maps results back to tickets by line number,
so it covers interleaved passes and failures across two patterns, two tickets
sharing one bad value, a format field keeping its note rather than its
pattern, and edit — the caller with a single ticket and no bulk to batch over
— still settling its own queue, because a finding deferred and never flushed
would save a bad value silently. Both failure modes verified by
reintroducing them: dropping the flush makes edit accept the bad value, and
an off-by-one in the mapping accuses the wrong ticket. The process count is
asserted to be equal at 20 and 60 tickets, which is the shape of the
regression rather than a number that every future check would move.
